        "lem-open-subsets-of-r-are-countable-unions-of-rational-intervals",
        "thm-heine-borel-r"
      ],
      "hypothesis_verification": "Use the locally-compact Euclidean corollary at n=1 and the metric Hausdorff theorem. For every open U subset R, the exact rational-interval lemma writes U as a countable union of (a_j,b_j). Each interval is the union over k>=0 of compact [a_j+(b_j-a_j)/(k+3), b_j-(b_j-a_j)/(k+3)], by Heine-Borel; the resulting countable family proves every open is sigma-compact (including empty U). Each scalar F_x is finite by the exact scalar-PVM lemma, independently of regularity, so there is no circularity.",
      "same_step_moment_clarity": "The current text says taking u=u_n and monotone convergence gives the lambda^2 moment. Use v_n(lambda)=lambda^2*1_{|lambda|<=n} for that scalar change-of-variables identity, then monotone convergence; bounded f makes the resulting second moment finite. This is a correction within Proof7.1 and preserves the Statement.",
      "public_interface_change_required": false,
      "statement_preserved": true,
      "assigned_consumer_current_use": "thm-nrqm-uncertainty-joint-pvm-criterion F2 and Proof1.3 consume only clause(a), which its existing proof establishes before the faulty7.1 uniqueness use. The proposed completion supplies clause(c) without altering(a)/(b), AC hypotheses or norms."
